Z||000007||mrfixit58||07-06-2000||07:28 PM||||I got to look at the time slips before they disappeared and from what I could see, his poor times may be more a factor of his driving then the motor. If you got to see his reaction times, they were all around 1.5 seconds. I don't know how that factors into the total time (maybe someone with track experience can let us know) but I always hear about reaction times when a driver loses a race.<P>He also mentioned how modest he went on the cam design. What about his ignition timing? If it's not set with enough advance, the motor will not make much power (I saw this on my 2.8 and my 1970 SS 454 Monte Carlo). He also mentioned he was still trying to find his shift points. And, what about his ECM? Could it handle the modifications?<P>It would be interesting to see what the motor could do if a real engine tuner got hold of it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||HYPTNOTiSE||07-06-2000||07:49 PM||||reaction time has nothing to do with 1/4 timeslips, but a bad reaction will cause you to loose a race.  In a bracket race, if both cars run their dial in exactly the only edge the cars have on each other is reaction time.  But as far as the time goes you could have a 3.0 light and a the next time .500(perfect) and if the car/drive performs exactly the same the times will be the same.  The isuzu tranny may make some difference, but not a lot. half a second at the very very most.  He needs a fuel pressure gauge and an a/f meter for starters.  Also a scan tool would be useful to find the injectors duty-cylce and find 02 readings as well. but 15.0??? that's dog tired for a 3.4.  If my 3.2 runs a 15 second 1/4 mile, i'd go to the store and buy a for sale sign. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula 18||07-07-2000||11:04 AM||||The thing I don't understand is that this guys spent about 3 grand in High-Pro goodies and machiene work so it is not like the engine came out of a van with 200K on it. Maybe he is just a REALLY sh!ty driver.||||reg||1
Z||000010||BBBAD GT||07-07-2000||01:58 PM||||What was his 1/8 & 1/4 mph?  Usually, mph shows how much HP you have, and ET is a function of how you use it.  If he did &gt;95 mph, then I'd say he's doing OK if thats all he did was 3.4 swap with stock heads and average weight for Fiero.<P><P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
